WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.342 --> 00:00:05.800
SPEAKER: In this
video, we're going

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.175
to go through how
you can migrate

00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:08.860
a workload-- in this
case, the payment

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.410
service for an online store
from a virtual machine

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.690
to a GKE Autopilot cluster.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.730
Before I dive into
the core of the demo,

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.830
let me say this demo
assumes you are already

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.490
familiar with both Migrate
for GKE and GKE Autopilot.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.638
If you're not, you might
want to pause the video

00:00:23.638 --> 00:00:25.930
and go get a quick overview
of both of these offerings,

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.860
and then come back
and rejoin me.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.360
With that being
said, let's go ahead

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.549
and take a look
at where we're at.

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:34.000
This is our initial
architecture.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.660
You can see the payment service
is there on the left in blue,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.130
and it's running in a VM.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.260
When we're done,
that will be migrated

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.270
to a container like
all the other services

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.975
in the online boutique.

00:00:43.975 --> 00:00:46.350
So let's go ahead and take a
look at that online boutique

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.827
and see it actually in action.

00:00:47.827 --> 00:00:49.410
So I'm going to go
here, and I'm going

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.490
to click on this barista set.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.323
I'm going to add to
cart, and I'm just going

00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:53.580
to go down and place my order.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.280
And you can see everything
works as expected.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.170
Lastly, before we jump
into the migration,

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.212
I want to show you the
infrastructure real quick.

00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:01.800
I have two GKE
clusters, one running

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.890
Migrate for GKE, the other
running the sample application.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.930
You can see that's
an Autopilot cluster.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.402
Now, if I click
on Workloads, you

00:01:08.402 --> 00:01:10.110
can see all of the
services running there

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.988
for the online boutique.

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:13.530
To perform the actual
migration, I'll

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.480
start by running an
analysis using Migrate

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.540
for GKE's migration fit tool
to see if our workload is

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.370
a good candidate for migration.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.200
Next, I'll create
a migration plan,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.210
and I edit it to
specify that we want

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.890
to enable the
resulting container

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.050
to run on GKE Autopilot.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.390
From there, I'll execute
the actual migration.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.530
This will result in
a set of artifacts,

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.470
including a Docker
file, a container image,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.360
and a pair of YAML files--

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.210
one to deploy the Kubernetes
services and deployment

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.400
and another to manage
the configuration

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.020
of those primitives.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.880
I'll then deploy the
newly migrated workload

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.320
using the Kubernetes YAML.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.480
Finally, I'll edit
the configuration

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.250
for the currently
running services

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.040
to point them to the newly
deployed Kubernetes service,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.970
replacing the virtual machine's
DNS name with the Kubernetes

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.670
service name.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.440
The first step in
any migration process

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.570
should be to ensure that
the targeted workload is

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.160
a good candidate for migration.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.650
With the 1.8 release
of Migrate for GKE,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.620
we've introduced via public
preview a new migration fit

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.700
assessment tool.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.670
This tool can operate against
VMs running on VMware vSphere

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.430
by directly interacting
with the vSphere APIs

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.070
or it can be run directly
against a single VM.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.720
In the case of our
demo today, I'm

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.270
operating directly on
the VM I want to migrate,

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.030
which happens to be running
on Google Compute Engine.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.660
But like I said, it could just
as easily be running on VMware.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.910
Additionally,
Migrate for GKE also

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:37.973
supports migrating workloads
from AWS and Azure.

00:02:37.973 --> 00:02:40.140
Let's go ahead and take a
look at the fit assessment

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.195
tool in action.

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:42.570
I'll run the fit
assessment tool,

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.612
which will gather information
about the processes

00:02:44.612 --> 00:02:46.560
and services running on my VM.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.460
Then I'll import
the resulting data,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.910
which is stored in a TAR
file into the fit assessment

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.060
database.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.780
I can use the ls command
to get a list of VMs

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.900
that I've gathered
data from previously.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.290
Next, I'll have the
fit tool analyze

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.880
the collected information.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.650
Once it's finished
its assessment,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.330
I will have it produce a
report on its findings.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.050
The report can be
in HTML or JSON.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.930
I'm going to specify JSON, so
I can upload it to the Google

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.310
Cloud console later.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.970
Also, while I'm only analyzing
a single VM in this demo,

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:21.698
you can batch process
this analysis.

00:03:21.698 --> 00:03:23.240
Now that I have the
report, I'm going

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.130
to upload it to the
Google Cloud console,

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:26.750
and you can see the result here.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.170
My application is a
good fit for migration.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.660
If I want to see exactly
what was found on the VM,

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.410
I can drill down and take a look
here at some of the details.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.620
Before I can actually migrate
a VM, I need to stop it.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.470
So let me move into the
shell here and issue the stop

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:43.407
instances command.

00:03:43.407 --> 00:03:45.990
I'll speed up the video a little
bit and when that's finished,

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.237
we can move on.

00:03:47.237 --> 00:03:49.070
So I've already created
a processing cluster

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.800
and defined a source, so
let's define our migration.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.630
We'll click Create Migration.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.165
I'm going to call
it paymentservice.

00:03:55.165 --> 00:03:57.290
We're going to select the
source I created earlier.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.410
It's Linux, and the VM
name is paymentservicevm.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.210
We're going to use
the image intent.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:05.920
We'll click Create Migration
and wait for that to finish.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.480
All right, now, we can move on.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.170
So let's go in and edit
the actual migration plan.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.670
Now, I need to make
a change in here

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.890
to ensure that the
resulting containers can

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:14.940
run on Autopilot.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.029
So I'll come in and I'm going
to add an annotation up here

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:18.779
near the top.

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:22.002
So I'll hit Enter and I'll
paste the annotation in.

00:04:22.002 --> 00:04:23.460
Then I'm going to
scroll down and I

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.470
want to change the
service in the image name

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.530
from paymentservicevm
to just paymentservice.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.680
So we'll change it here
and we'll change it here.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.120
We'll click Generate Artifacts.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.620
That will take a
few minutes, so I'm

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.995
going to speed up this video and
rejoin you when it's finished.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.760
The migration is finished and
I've downloaded the resulting

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.680
artifacts into Cloud
Shell, so let's take

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.440
a look at the config.yaml file.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.870
The config.yaml file
is used to control

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.180
a bunch of different aspects
of the container that's

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.670
going to run, including
what processes are running

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.220
in the container, some
things about the file system

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.240
and how it's laid out.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.350
Now, I'm not going to
spend a lot of time here.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.230
I just wanted you to
be aware of this file

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.060
because you may have to
edit it in the future.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.650
So let's go ahead and apply the
deployment_spec.yaml that was

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:10.410
generated.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.070
This is going to create
two Kubernetes primitives,

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:15.030
the service and the deployment.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.970
So let's go ahead and
take a look at the pods

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.160
and see if our new service
is up and running yet.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.720
It looks like it's
actually still pending,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.387
so I'm going to pause
the video and I'll

00:05:26.387 --> 00:05:30.120
rejoin you once it's started.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.480
OK, it's been a few minutes.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.410
Let's clear the screen
and take another look.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.300
And it looks like our
pod is up and running.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.960
So let's go ahead now, we'll
clear the screen again,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.640
and then what we're
going to do is

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.132
we're going to go ahead and
check out the online boutique.

00:05:44.132 --> 00:05:45.590
Let's go ahead and
get the services

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.810
so we can get that
external IP address.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.778
And we'll copy that up,
and we'll paste it in here,

00:05:50.778 --> 00:05:52.070
and let's try to buy something.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:56.360
So the camera, add to cart,
complete the purchase.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:57.540
You see an error.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.500
Now, that's because while I
have the new service running,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.810
my VM is stopped and
the application is still

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:04.020
trying to access it.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.550
So what I need to do is I need
to edit the deployment spec

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.710
for the actual application.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.170
And right here, you
can see where we set

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:13.560
that payment service address.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.220
So let me change that from
the DNS name of the VM

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.890
to the service name of
the Kubernetes service.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:21.020
I'll save that out.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.000
We'll go back into
the command line,

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.790
and I'll apply that
deployment spec so

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:29.150
that we can update the
services to look not at the VM,

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.680
but at that new container
we just deployed.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.740
So we'll apply that out.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.300
You can see all
the configuration

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.280
changes coming along here.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.230
And now, I'm going to
go ahead, once again,

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:42.300
make sure that the
pods have started up.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.550
Looks like the pod has
started up successfully.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.415
So let's go ahead and move
back to the online boutique,

00:06:47.415 --> 00:06:49.040
and let's try to buy
that camera again.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.750
So we'll click Add to Cart.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.200
We'll click Place Order, and
everything works as expected.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:55.700
We have migrated
the payment service

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.530
from the virtual
machine to a container

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.460
running on GKE Autopilot.

